docs(#70): settle the three harness enforcement-mechanism questions

Three design decisions in the enforcement layer (epic #64) were being made
on assumption. Ran each test live in this same headless sandcastle session
rather than reading documentation:

1. Background-task notifications do arrive headless — a run_in_background
   task's completion notification landed unprompted, no polling. Keep the
   heartbeat/anti-polling blocklist entries; the premise behind them holds.

2. Deny rules do survive permission-bypass mode — a temporary
   `permissions.deny` entry in .claude/settings.json blocked a matching
   Bash call outright, live, with no restart, even though this session
   otherwise runs with permissions bypassed. But deny matches by command
   prefix, and most real recorded commands are compound, so a shell guard
   is still needed for coverage; deny rules are the better instrument only
   for the subset of unambiguous, non-compound single-command patterns.

3. Local settings cannot remove a tracked deny — adding a contradicting
   `allow` for the same pattern to the untracked .claude/settings.local.json
   did not unblock it. Permission arrays merge as a union across sources
   and deny wins regardless of source, so a tracked-settings deny strategy
   survives an untracked local file. Also confirmed directly that neither
   this repo's .gitignore nor .git/info/exclude reference
   .claude/settings.local.json, matching the issue's note that it's kept
   out of version control only by the user's personal global gitignore.

All temporary test config was reverted; settings.json and
settings.local.json diff clean against their pre-test state. This is a
spike — no code changes. Posting the answers to the issue/epic is left to
the host process after this run, per the sandbox's no-GitHub-access rule.
EOF
